1. Declare without initialising
int a, b, c;
This creates three int variables. Their values are not defined yet, so we assign them before we read them:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int a, b, c;
    a = 1; b = 2; c = 3;
    printf("a=%d b=%d c=%d\n", a, b, c);
    return 0;
}
a=1 b=2 c=3

2. Declare and initialise each one
Every variable can get its own starting value, and a later one may use an earlier one in the same line:
int x = 10, y = 20, z = x + y;
double price = 9.99, tax = 0.15, total = price * (1 + tax);
printf("x=%d y=%d z=%d\n", x, y, z);
printf("total=%.2f\n", total);
x=10 y=20 z=30
total=11.49
z = x + y works because x and y are already declared (and given values) by the time the compiler reaches z.

The traps

Partial initialisation
int a, b = 5;
Only b is set to 5. The = belongs to one variable, not to the whole line. a holds whatever was in that memory before, and the compiler warns when we read it:
warning: 'a' is used uninitialized [-Wuninitialized]
A common and harmless use is a loop counter, because the loop itself gives i its first value:
int i, sum = 0;
for (i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    sum += i;
}
printf("sum = %d\n", sum);
sum = 45


The asterisk belongs to the variable
int *r, s;
Here r is a pointer to int, but s is a plain int. We can see the difference in their sizes:
int *r, s;
s = 4; r = &s;
printf("sizeof(r)=%zu sizeof(s)=%zu\n", sizeof(r), sizeof(s));
sizeof(r)=8 sizeof(s)=4
(8 bytes is a pointer on a 64-bit machine.) Writing int* r, s; looks as if both are pointers, but it means exactly the same thing: only r is. To make both pointers we repeat the asterisk:
int *r, *s;
If we later write p = q; with int *p, q;, gcc complains: assignment to ‘int *’ from ‘int’ makes pointer from integer without a cast. That message often means the asterisk trap.
Declarations can also mix a plain variable, an array and a pointer, since the extra symbols attach to each name:
int n = 7, arr[3] = {1, 2, 3}, *ptr = &n;
printf("n=%d arr[1]=%d *ptr=%d\n", n, arr[1], *ptr);
n=7 arr[1]=2 *ptr=7
That compiles, but it is a good example of a line we would rather split.


You cannot mix base types
int a, float b;
error: expected identifier or '(' before 'float'
Each statement has one base type. Use two statements: int a; float b;.

Should we do it?
Both styles are correct. Many programmers keep one variable per line:
int count = 0;
int total = 0;
It is easier to comment each variable, simpler to read in a diff under version control, and it avoids the pointer and partial-initialisation traps. A single line is fine for closely related values of the same kind, such as int row, col; or double x = 0, y = 0;.

A modern alternative: declare in the loop
Since C99, a loop counter can be declared inside the for itself. It then exists only inside the loop and cannot leak out:
int sum = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 10; i++) {
    sum += i;
}

Code
 5 min

By the end of this lesson you will
Fill a fixed-size array from a loop
Grow an array with malloc and realloc




A for loop writes i * i into results[i] on each pass, filling the array one slot at a time.


To save loop results in an array in C, we declare an array with a fixed size before the loop, then use the loop’s index variable to assign a value to each slot.
Unlike Python, a C array cannot grow with something like append(). We manage the indexing ourselves.

A fixed-size array
This program squares the numbers 0 to 4 inside a loop and stores each result:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

#define SIZE 5   // fixed size of the array

int main(void) {
    // 1. Declare the array with a predefined size
    int results[SIZE];

    // 2. Use a loop to populate the array
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; SIZE; i++) {
        results[i] = i * i;   // save the result at index i
    }

    // 3. Print the saved elements to check
    printf("Saved results:\n");
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; SIZE; i++) {
        printf("results[%d] = %d\n", i, results[i]);
    }

    return 0;
}
Output (compiled with gcc -Wall -Wextra):
Saved results:
results[0] = 0
results[1] = 1
results[2] = 4
results[3] = 9
results[4] = 16






Warning



Stay inside the array. Valid indices run from 0 to SIZE - 1, so the loop condition must be i &lt; SIZE, not i &lt;= SIZE. Writing outside the array is undefined behaviour: it may crash, or silently corrupt other variables.




When the number of values is unknown
If we do not know in advance how many times the loop will run (for example, reading numbers until the user types 0), a fixed-size array does not work. We allocate memory with malloc() and enlarge it with realloc() when it fills up:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;
#include &lt;stdlib.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int capacity = 2, count = 0, x;
    int *data = malloc(capacity * sizeof *data);
    if (data == NULL) return 1;

    printf("Enter numbers (0 to stop): ");
    while (scanf("%d", &x) == 1 && x != 0) {
        if (count == capacity) {          // full: double the space
            capacity *= 2;
            int *tmp = realloc(data, capacity * sizeof *data);
            if (tmp == NULL) { free(data); return 1; }
            data = tmp;
        }
        data[count++] = x;                // save, then move to the next slot
    }

    printf("Saved %d values:", count);
    for (int i = 0; i &lt; count; i++) printf(" %d", data[i]);
    printf("\n");

    free(data);
    return 0;
}
Entering 4 8 15 16 23 42 0 gives:
Enter numbers (0 to stop): Saved 6 values: 4 8 15 16 23 42
Three habits to notice:

count tracks how many slots are used, and doubles as the next index.
realloc() goes into a temporary pointer first, so we do not lose data if it fails.
free() returns the memory when we are done.



Summary







Situation
Approach




Number of iterations known
Fixed array, array[i] = value;


Number of iterations unknown
malloc() + realloc(), track count and capacity







