A simple example
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int toss = 1;

    switch (toss) {
        case 1:
            printf("Head\n");
            break;
        case 2:
            printf("Tail\n");
            break;
    }

    return 0;
}
Output:
Head
C evaluates toss once, jumps to the case whose value matches, and runs from there until it meets a break.

Example 2: a menu
A menu selection is the classic use of switch:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int choice;

    printf("--- Simple Menu ---\n");
    printf("1. Check Balance\n");
    printf("2. Deposit Money\n");
    printf("3. Withdraw Money\n");
    printf("Enter your choice (1-3): ");
    scanf("%d", &choice);

    // The switch expression must evaluate to an integer or character
    switch (choice) {
        case 1:
            printf("Your current balance is $1,500.\n");
            break;  // exits the switch block

        case 2:
            printf("Enter the amount you wish to deposit.\n");
            break;

        case 3:
            printf("Enter the amount you wish to withdraw.\n");
            break;

        default:
            // Runs if 'choice' doesn't match any case above
            printf("Invalid selection! Please enter a number between 1 and 3.\n");
            break;
    }

    return 0;
}
Sample run (entering 2):
--- Simple Menu ---
1. Check Balance
2. Deposit Money
3. Withdraw Money
Enter your choice (1-3): 2
Enter the amount you wish to deposit.

Fall-through: the classic bug (and a useful trick)
Forgetting break is the most common switch mistake:
int n = 1;

switch (n) {
    case 1:
        printf("one\n");      // no break!
    case 2:
        printf("two\n");      // runs too
    case 3:
        printf("three\n");    // and this
        break;
    default:
        printf("other\n");
}
Output:
one
two
three
The match is case 1, but with no break the program keeps running through case 2 and case 3 until the first break. Compiling with -Wall -Wextra (or -Wimplicit-fallthrough) makes GCC warn about this.
Fall-through can also be useful on purpose, to give several cases the same code. Here we simply leave the shared cases empty:
char grade = 'B';

switch (grade) {
    case 'A':
    case 'B':
        printf("Good\n");     // A and B share this
        break;
    case 'C':
        printf("Okay\n");
        break;
    default:
        printf("Unknown grade\n");
}
Output: Good. When fall-through is intentional and the cases do contain code, add a comment such as /* fall through */ (or the [[fallthrough]]; attribute in C23) so the next reader knows it isn’t a mistake.

switch with characters and enums
Characters are integers in C, so case 'A' works. enum values are a natural fit as well, since the compiler can warn if we forget one:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

enum Color { RED, GREEN, BLUE };

int main(void) {
    enum Color c = GREEN;

    switch (c) {
        case RED:   puts("red");   break;
        case GREEN: puts("green"); break;
        case BLUE:  puts("blue");  break;
    }
    return 0;
}
This prints green. If we later add YELLOW to the enum and don’t handle it, -Wall will warn that the switch doesn’t cover all values (when there is no default).

Declaring variables inside a case
A case label is not a block, so declaring a variable directly after one can fail to compile in older C standards, and the variable’s scope covers the later cases. Wrap the case body in braces:
switch (x) {
    case 2: {
        int square = x * x;
        printf("%d\n", square);
        break;
    }
    default:
        break;
}

1. Start Simple: Drop the Braces
Here is a very common piece of code, inside a loop that should skip the number 4. The long form:
if (i == 4) {
    continue;
}
The braces { } are needed only to group several statements. When the body is a single statement, we can leave them out, and the whole if fits on one line:
if (i == 4) continue;
Same meaning, two lines shorter. In a complete program:
for (int i = 1; i &lt;= 6; i++) {
    if (i == 4) continue;
    printf("%d ", i);
}
printf("\n");
1 2 3 5 6 
The number 4 is skipped. (Section 5 shows the one trap of this shortcut.)

2. The Ternary Operator ? :
An if-else that only chooses a value can shrink too. The pattern is condition ? value_if_true : value_if_false. It is an expression: it produces a value we can assign, return or print. First, the long form:
int a = 7, b = 12;
int max;
if (a &gt; b) {
    max = a;
} else {
    max = b;
}
The shortcut:
int max = a &gt; b ? a : b;
Both give 12. Read the shortcut aloud as “is a greater than b? then a, otherwise b”. Together in a runnable program:
#include &lt;stdio.h&gt;

int main(void) {
    int a = 7, b = 12;
    int max = a &gt; b ? a : b;
    printf("%d\n", max);
    return 0;
}
12

Example: choose a word inside printf
Because it is an expression, it fits inside a function call, which is where it is most useful:
for (int n = 3; n &lt;= 4; n++) {
    printf("%d is %s\n", n, n % 2 == 0 ? "even" : "odd");
}
int items = 1;
printf("%d item%s\n", items, items == 1 ? "" : "s");
items = 3;
printf("%d item%s\n", items, items == 1 ? "" : "s");
3 is odd
4 is even
1 item
3 items
The second pattern, adding an s only when the count is not 1, is a classic.


Example: absolute value and clamping
int n = -5, low = 0, high = 10, x = 15;
int abs_n = n &lt; 0 ? -n : n;
int clamped = x &lt; low ? low : (x &gt; high ? high : x);
printf("abs = %d, clamped = %d\n", abs_n, clamped);
abs = 5, clamped = 10
The second line nests a ternary inside another: below low gives low, above high gives high, otherwise x. One level of nesting is fine; beyond that, write a normal if.






Warning



Keep it readable. A chain such as score &gt;= 80 ? 'A' : score &gt;= 60 ? 'B' : 'C' works (it printed A for 85), but three or more levels are harder to read than the if/else if they replace. The ternary is a shortcut for a simple choice between two values. It is not a replacement for if when the branches do things (several statements, loops, return).

4. A Comparison Is a Number
A comparison gives 1 when true and 0 when false, so it can be stored or added:
int year = 2100;
int leap = (year % 4 == 0 && year % 100 != 0) || year % 400 == 0;
printf("%d leap? %d\n", year, leap);

int a = 5, b = 3;
printf("a &gt; b gives %d, a &lt; b gives %d\n", a &gt; b, a &lt; b);

// counting with a condition: no if needed
int nums[] = {4, 9, 2, 7, 10, 3}, big = 0;
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 6; i++) {
    big += nums[i] &gt; 5;
}
printf("numbers above 5: %d\n", big);
2100 leap? 0
a &gt; b gives 1, a &lt; b gives 0
numbers above 5: 3
big += nums[i] &gt; 5; adds 1 for every element above 5 and 0 otherwise, replacing if (nums[i] &gt; 5) big++;. (2100 is not a leap year: divisible by 100 but not by 400.)

5. The Trap of Braces-Free if
The shortcut in section 1 works because the body is a single statement. The trap is adding a second one later. Only the first statement after the if belongs to it, whatever the indentation says:
int n = -1;
if (n &gt; 0)
    printf("one\n");
    printf("two (always printed!)\n");
two (always printed!)
n is not positive, yet the second printf runs, because it is not part of the if. With -Wall, gcc even warns: this 'if' clause does not guard... [-Wmisleading-indentation]. The fix is to keep the braces whenever the body might grow:
if (n &gt; 0) {
    printf("one\n");
    printf("two\n");
}

6. Lookup Instead of if/else if
When a number picks one of several fixed texts, an array beats a chain of ifs:
int month = 3;
const char *name[] = {"Jan", "Feb", "Mar", "Apr"};
printf("%s\n", name[month - 1]);
Mar

Precedence and Type Pitfalls
int x = 4;
int r = x % 2 == 0 ? 10 : 20;
int p = 1 + x &gt; 3 ? 100 : 200;   // 1 + x is computed, then &gt; 3, then ?:
printf("%d %d\n", r, p);
printf("%.1f\n", 5 &gt; 3 ? 1.5 : 2);
10 100
1.5

?: has low precedence: the whole condition is evaluated first. When in doubt, add parentheses around the condition, and around a ternary used inside a bigger expression.
If the two values have different types (1.5 and 2), the result takes the common type (double). Use the matching format (%.1f, not %d).

Summary








Shortcut
Replaces
Use when




x = c ? a : b;
if (c) x = a; else x = b;
choosing one of two values


printf("%s", c ? "yes" : "no");
an if around two printfs
picking text inside output


if (x) / if (!x)
x != 0 / x == 0
flags and counts


count += cond;
if (cond) count++;
counting matches


if (c) stmt;
if (c) { stmt; }
one short statement, no later additions


name[i]
a chain of else if
an index selects a fixed value

